    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-13-9971-2013",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Estimating anthropogenic aerosol effects on the planetary energy balance through the aerosol influence on clouds using the difference in cloud radiative forcing from simulations with and without anthropogenic emissions produces estimates that are positively biased. A more representative method is suggested using the difference in cloud radiative forcing calculated as a diagnostic with aerosol scattering and absorption neglected. The method also yields an aerosol radiative forcing decomposition that includes a term quantifying the impact of changes in surface albedo. The method requires only two additional diagnostic calculations: the whole-sky and clear-sky top-of-atmosphere radiative flux with aerosol scattering and absorption neglected.",
    "field": "Aerosol-cloud-radiation interactions",
    "problem": "Existing methods for estimating aerosol effects on cloud radiative forcing are positively biased because they incorrectly account for direct aerosol-radiation interactions, such as the warming enhancement by absorbing aerosols situated above clouds and exaggerated cooling by scattering aerosols.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a method to estimate aerosol effects on cloud radiative forcing using the difference in cloud forcing calculated from diagnostic radiation calls that neglect aerosol scattering and absorption to correct a positive bias in conventional estimates.",
    "task": "The task is to propose a more representative method for estimating aerosol effects on cloud radiative forcing that corrects a positive bias inherent in the conventional approach of differencing simulations with and without anthropogenic emissions.",
    "model": [
        "CAM5.1",
        "CESM",
        "NICAM"
    ],
